{
  "summary": "The terminal_stage proposal is sound for truncation use cases, but the previous critique about skipping early phases reveals a real configuration gap that deserves a unified solution rather than a second config knob. Instead of fragmenting the model with both terminal_stage and skip_phases/start_stage, I propose a single 'active_phases' list in board config that declares which of the three natural pipeline blocks run: ['plan', 'spec', 'impl']. This is not arbitrary phase composition — it's a bounded enum of three blocks that already exist as structurally separated code in _run_pipeline_impl (lines 1485-1576 for planning, 1578-1613 for spec writing, 1615-1616 for impl/test/review). The implementation is minimal: after each block completes, check whether the next block is in active_phases; if not, route to final-human-approval. This preserves the human checkpoint that round 4 correctly identified as essential, handles both truncation (active_phases: ['plan']) and skip-early (active_phases: ['impl'] for pre-planned items) cases with one config field, and requires no refactoring of the phase-internal retry/branching logic. The key insight the prior rounds missed is that the spec writing block at line 1578 has NO stage gate — it runs unconditionally after plan review, meaning a terminal_stage check after plan review would need to be inserted before that ungated code, not after it. An active_phases approach naturally handles this because the gate is 'is spec in active_phases?' rather than 'did we just finish the terminal stage?', making the check location unambiguous. For skip-early cases, items would need to be placed directly into the correct starting stage (e.g., 'approved' for spec-only, 'implementing' for impl-only), which the existing move_to_stage mechanism already supports."
}
